#format http://ns.nuke24.net/TOGETL/TEF/Formats/SyntaxV0.3

This is a request list, kept as a TEF file as an own-dogfood-eating exercise.

Design decisions live in PLAN.md; this file is the execution backlog.

Keep this file concise, actionable, and acceptance-oriented.

The tef:content of each `=task` is notes.  You might think of it as being the `dc:description`.

As with many TEF files, the preamble before the first `=`-entry
contains information about the file as a whole.  But now the preamble
is explaining about the pre-amble.  Let's move on.



=task S36CR-001
status: pending
priority: high
kind: design
scope: PLAN.md; Java source files
title: Java naming conventions
request: Use UpperCamelCase for type names and lowerCamelCase for variables/methods; apply the convention consistently across the Java codebase.
acceptance: All active identifiers follow the project's actual Java naming rule, without ad hoc exceptions.

The original wording was confusing; this should be captured as an explicit convention rather than left implicit.

- For UpperCamelCase, upper-case the first letter of each word.
  If a word is ALLUPPERCASE, it stays that way.
- For lowerCamelCase, lower-case everything, *then* upper-case the first letter
  of all but the first word.  'XYZ TLA factory' becomes `xyzTlaFactory`

=task S36CR-002
status: done
priority: high
kind: design
scope: PLAN.md; vocabulary and identifiers
title: Namespace split for invented terms vs parsed data
request: Use tag URIs for both TOGETL vocabulary terms and domain data, with separate namespaces under `tag:nuke24.net,2026-11`: `togetl` for TOGETL itself and a more specific domain namespace such as `3dparts` for data-level IDs.
acceptance: The design clearly distinguishes project vocabulary terms from temporary/derived data identifiers while keeping both under the same tag: URI authority and date.

Implementations reserve `tag:nuke24.net,2026-11:togetl:` for ontology and infrastructure terms, and `tag:nuke24.net,2026-11:3dparts:` for part/data-derived resources such as STL files and renderings.

=task S36CR-003
status: done
priority: high
kind: design
scope: PLAN.md; mapping vocabulary
title: Remove Uri/Ref from term names
request: Do not encode literal-vs-URI distinction into term names; model it as the value structure instead.
acceptance: Term names describe concepts, not representation details; RDF mappings reflect the actual literal/IRI distinction.

Don't include "Uri" or "Ref" in term names, since, when translated to RDF,
whether the value is a literal or a URI reference is part of the value.
e.g. "doc-ref: http://foo" should probably be mapped to "documentedBy <http://foo>" (in turtle-ese).
i.e. the resource referenced is not itself a URI!

Or, put another way:
TEF has no way of indicating how the value part of a header should be interpreted.
This is why some headers are named like `doc-ref` or `somesuch-url`;
the value is a [URI] reference.  If we interpret the value as a reference
*and* keep the "ref" part of the name, we're kind of implying the thing
needs to be double-deferenced, which is wrong.

